Working of HashMap in JavaLast Updated : 28 Mar 2026What is HashingHashing is the process of converting an object into an integer value. This integer value helps in indexing and enables faster searching.In other words, hashing is a technique used to uniquely identify an object from a group of similar objects.What is HashMapHashMap is a part of the Java Collection Framework. It uses a technique called hashing and implements the Map interface.It stores data in the form of key-value pairs. Internally, HashMap uses an array of nodes, where each node represents a key-value pair. It uses an array and LinkedList data structure for storing data.Working of HashMap in JavaBefore understanding the internal working of HashMap, we must know about hashCode() and equals() methods.equals():It checks whether two objects are equal or not by comparing their keys. It is a method of the Object class and can be overridden. If you override equals(), then it is mandatory to override hashCode() as well.hashCode():It is a method of the Object class that returns an integer value for the object. This value is used to determine the bucket location in HashMap. The hash code of a null key is always 0.Buckets:Buckets are an array of nodes used in HashMap. Each bucket can store multiple nodes using a LinkedList structure. More than one element can be stored in the same bucket.Hash FunctionA hash function takes an input (or key) and returns a fixed-length value, usually an integer called a hash code.This hash code is used to index data in a hash table (like the array used in HashMap). The main goal of a hash function is to distribute data evenly and reduce collisions.Insert Key, Value Pair in HashMapWe use the put() method to insert key-value pairs into a HashMap. By default, HashMap has an initial capacity of 16 (index range 0 to 15).ExampleIn this example, we are inserting three key-value pairs into a HashMap.HashMap<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put("Aman", 19);
map.put("Sunny", 29);
map.put("Ritesh", 39);
When we call the put() method, it calculates the hash code of the key. For example, suppose the hash code of "Aman" is 2657860. To store the key in memory, an index is calculated.Calculating IndexThe index is used to determine the position where the key-value pair will be stored in the array.The formula for calculating the index is:	Index = hashcode(Key) & (n-1)
Where n is the size of the array.So, for the key "Aman":Index = 2657860 & (16-1) = 4
The value 4 is the computed index value where the Key and value will store in HashMap.Hash CollisionThis is the case when the calculated index value is the same for two or more Keys. Let's calculate the hash code for another Key, "Sunny." Suppose, the hash code for "Sunny" is 63281940. To store the Key in the memory, we have to calculate the index by using the index formula.Index=63281940 & (16-1) = 4
The value 4 is the computed index value where the Key will be stored in HashMap. In this case, equals() method check that both Keys are equal or not. If Keys are same, replace the value with the current value. Otherwise, connect this node object to the existing node object through the LinkedList. Hence both Keys will be stored at index 4.Similarly, we will store the Key "Ritesh." Suppose, hash code for the Key is 2349873. The index value will be 1. Hence, this Key will be stored at index 1.Handling CollisionsDespite efforts to minimize them, collisions are inevitable. Two main strategies to handle collisions are:Chaining: Storing all elements that hash to the same index in a linked list or tree at thatOpen Addressing: Finding another open slot within the hash table by probing (for example, linear probing, quadratic probing) when a collision occurs.HashMap.get() MethodThe get() method is used to get the value by its Key. It will not fetch the value if we do not know the Key. When the get(K Key) method is called, it calculates the hash code of the Key.Suppose, we have to fetch the Key "Aman." The following method will be called.	map.get(new Key("Aman"));
It generates the hash code as 2657860. Now calculate the index value of 2657860 by using index formula. The index value will be 4, as we have calculated above. The get() method search for the index value 4. It compares the first element Key with the given Key. If both keys are equal, it returns the value else check for the next element in the node if it exists. In our scenario, it is found as the first element of the node and return the value 19.Let's fetch another Key "Sunny."The hash code of the Key "Sunny" is 63281940. The calculated index value of 63281940 is 4, as we have calculated for put() method. Go to index 4 of the array and compare the first element's Key with the given Key. It also compares Keys. In our scenario, the given Key is the second element, and the next of the node is null. It compares the second element Key with the specified Key and returns the value 29. It returns null if the next of the node is null.HashMap TraversalJava provides various methods such as entrySet(), keySet(), and values() to traverse over hash map. These methods allow us to iterate over the keys, values, or key-value pairs of the HashMap. Consider the following code snippet.for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : map.entrySet()) {
    System.out.println("Key: " + entry.getKey() + ", Value: " + entry.getValue());
}
Rehashing and Load FactorTo maintain performance, a HashMap needs to resize itself (rehash) when the number of elements grows significantly. It is controlled by the load factor that is a measure of how full the hash table is allowed to get before its capacity is automatically increased.Load Factor: HashMap's default load factor is 0.75. It means that when the hash table is 75% full, it will resize by doubling its current capacity.Rehashing: When rehashing occurs, a new, larger array is created, and all the existing entries are rehashed to new indices in the new array. It ensures that the hash table maintains a good balance between time and space complexity.Next TopicJava LinkedHashMap ← prev next →
